    "result_text": "You can now flag clients as key accounts, and the clients list shows a \"Key account\" badge next to their names. The acceptance test passes under `bin/e2e`.\n\nOne note: the request said \"OfficeFloor server\", but the server is Spring Boot. \"OfficeFloor\" only appears in the package name `net.officefloor.hq.app`, so I followed Spring conventions as `CLAUDE.md` says.\n\n- **Database:** `V42__client_key_account.sql` adds a `key_account` column to `client`, defaulting to false, so existing clients are not key accounts.\n- **Server:**\n  - The client record, the create/edit request and the client response all carry the new flag.\n  - Creating a client defaults it to off; editing a client leaves it unchanged if it isn't sent.\n  - The test seed endpoint (`/__test__/seed`) accepts `keyAccount`.\n- **Front end:**\n  - **Clients list:** a key account gets the badge (`data-testid=\"client-key-account\"`) beside its name; other clients get nothing.\n  - **Add and edit forms:** each has a \"Key account\" checkbox (`client-form-key-account` and `client-edit-form-key-account`).\n  - **Client detail page:** shows \"Key account: Yes/No\" (`client-key-account-flag`).\n\nI didn't rename or remove any existing `data-testid`, and the test only covers the badge on the list. Changing the flag through the edit form isn't written to the audit log as its own event; it falls under the existing general \"client updated\" record.",
